0	201	Ekaterina Spasova Gecheva-Zaharieva (Bulgarian: Екатерина Спасова Гечева-Захариева, born 8 August 1975) is a Bulgarian politician serving as European Commissioner for Startups, Research and Innovation
202	307	She served as Deputy Prime Minister of Bulgaria from 2017 to 2021 and on two occasions from 2013 to 2014.
308	566	A member of the GERB party, she also served as Minister of Foreign Affairs from 2017 to 2021, Minister of Justice from 2015 to 2017, Minister of Regional Development on two occasions from 2013 to 2014
566	632	Early life and education
Zaharieva was born in Pazardzhik in 1975.
633	750	She became fluent in German at her local college, then attended the University of Plovdiv where she graduated in law.
751	797	This was followed by a law master's degree.



797	839	Career

She worked as a lawyer until 2003.
840	913	She then became a legal Advisor of the Ministry of Environment and Water.
914	1002	Zaharieva became the Director of Legal, Administrative and Regulatory Services in 2007.

1002	1117	Entering politics in 2009, she became the Deputy Minister of Regional Development to the Minister Rosen Plevneliev.
1118	1257	When Plevneliev became President of Bulgaria, he chose her for the position of one of the Deputy Prime Ministers from March to May in 2013.
1258	1339	She was interim deputy Prime Minister to Marin Raykov in the Bulgarian Cabinet.


1339	1392	In 2013 she was the Minister of Regional Development.
1393	1471	From 18 December 2015 she served as Minister of Justice until 27 January 2017.
1472	1531	She became the Foreign Minister of Bulgaria on 4 May 2017.

1531	1684	In August 2024 she was nominated, alongside Julian Popov, by the Bulgarian Government to be the next commissioner in the second von der Leyen Commission.
1685	1783	Bulgaria was the only country to have followed Von der Leyen's request to nominate two candidates.
1784	1898	Zaharieva was chosen by von der Leyen and proposed as the next Commissioner for Startups, Research and Innovation.
1899	1992	On 27 November 2024, the European Parliament elected the College of Commissioners as a whole.
1993	2082	The newly appointed Commission is starting its five-year term as from 1 December 2024.



